Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 20:07:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [cert-manager] Bump to 1.20

cert-manager 1.18 supports Kubernetes 1.29-1.33 and went EOL on 2026-03-10.
OKD 4.21 is Kubernetes 1.34, so the operator built from this branch is outside
its supported Kubernetes range on the OKD version it ships alongside.

1.20 covers Kubernetes 1.32-1.35 = OKD 4.19 through 4.22, so one bump clears
both the current 4.21 and the upcoming 4.22. (1.21 is not an option: there is no
cert-manager-1.21 branch in openshift/cert-manager-operator.)

Two things beyond the version bump were required, both found by running the
build rather than by inspection:

1. patches/operator.patch had to be regenerated. Its hunk context contained
   'olm.skipRange: >=1.18.0 <1.18.1', so 'git am -3' failed against the 1.20 CSV
   base with "sha1 information is lacking or useless / could not build fake
   ancestor". The regenerated patch makes the same substitutions and applies
   cleanly to a fresh cert-manager-1.20 checkout.

2. The 1.20 Makefile added a validate-semver guard that 1.18 does not have, and
   it accepts only bare X.Y.Z. common.sh builds OCP_DATE as
   ${MAJOR}.${MINOR}.0-${DATE}, so 'make bundle BUNDLE_VERSION=1.20.0-<date>'
   hard-errors. The patch now also relaxes that guard to accept a semver
   pre-release suffix, which is what the version already is per semver.org --
   the guard is stricter than the spec it is named after. The date suffix is
   load-bearing here: the catalog already carries two builds of 1.18.0
   distinguished only by it.

Verified locally: 'make bundle BUNDLE_VERSION=1.20.0-2026-09-08-200000' runs to
completion and 'operator-sdk bundle validate ./bundle' reports "All validation
tests have completed successfully". Generated CSV is
cert-manager-operator.v1.20.0-2026-09-08-200000, advertises cert-manager v1.20.3,
and carries OKD branding.

Not verified: no container images were built (build host is arm64, target
amd64), and the bundle was not installed on a cluster.

Refs: https://cert-manager.io/docs/releases/
